{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa music \"anglo\" america (+ mainstream crossover market))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1916138507#12_2861514811","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2453","rank":1,"snippet":"meaning is not found in most stories of the term’s origin. ( See Salsa music for more information) HISTORY The history of “Salsa” dance is peppered with hearsay and contradiction. Although few would disagree that the music and dance forms originate largely in Cuban Son, most agree that Salsa as we know it today is a North American interpretation of the older forms. New York’s Latino community had a vibrant musical and dancing scene throughout the ’50s but found limited success with the ‘<cover>Anglo’ mainstream. In the 1970s, adoption of the term “Salsa” reduced the linguistic and cultural barriers to mainstream adoption of Latin music and dance. The modernization of the Mambo in the 1950s was influential in shaping what would become salsa. There is debate as to whether the dance we call Salsa today originated in Cuba or Puerto Rico. Cuba’s influence in North America </cover>was diminished after Castro’s revolution and the ensuing trade embargo.\" , \"start_char\": 7282.000000 , \"title\": \"What is Salsa History - Toronto Dance School | Salsa Lessons\" , \"url\": \"http://latinenergydance.com/what-is-salsa/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"4/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1916138507#12_2861514811","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly addresses why salsa had limited notice or success in mainstream North America, citing limited success with the Anglo mainstream, linguistic and cultural barriers, and diminished Cuban influence after Castro and the trade embargo.","learned":"A key explanation is that New York's Latino salsa scene had limited success with the Anglo mainstream because of linguistic/cultural barriers; the term 'salsa' helped reduce those barriers, while the Cuban embargo diminished Cuban influence."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2453","reason":"This query produced direct evidence using Anglo/mainstream/crossover terminology, so the next ranked results may contain more grade-3 explanations."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2453"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1916138507#13_2861517099","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2453","rank":2,"snippet":"Cuban style Los Angeles style (LA) New York style Famous On2 dancers Venezolana (Dominicana) style Colombian style Mambo Puerto Rican style Salsa Disco Salsa styling Shines *** THANK YOU FOR VISITING OUR SALSA PAGE! WE HOPE TO SEE YOU IN CLASS! *** \" , \"segment\": \"New York’s Latino community had a vibrant musical and dancing scene throughout the ’50s but found limited success with the ‘<cover>Anglo’ mainstream. In the 1970s, adoption of the term “Salsa” reduced the linguistic and cultural barriers to mainstream adoption of Latin music and dance. The modernization of the Mambo in the 1950s was influential in shaping what would become salsa. There is debate as to whether the dance we call Salsa today originated in Cuba or Puerto Rico. Cuba’s influence in North America </cover>was diminished after Castro’s revolution and the ensuing trade embargo. New York’s Latino community was largely Puerto-Rican. Salsa is one of the main dances in both Cuba and Puerto Rico and is known world-wide. Interpretations of what the salsa dance is The late Celia Cruz, hailed by many as the queen of salsa, said that salsa does not exist as a rhythm, but that "}}
