{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1360011309#11_2282375319","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage is salsa-music history and gives a potentially direct explanation: the Anglo market had abandoned Afro-Cuban/Latin music after mambo/cha-cha popularity faded, which helps explain why salsa was not noticed by mainstream America. However, the snippet is cut off and the answer is incomplete.","learned":"Another possible cause is that the Anglo market abandoned Latin/Afro-Cuban music after the mambo/cha-cha boom waned, before salsa labeling helped revive interest."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1360011309#11_2282375319","reason":"The snippet contains potentially grade-3 evidence but is truncated; the full document may clearly explain why salsa was unnoticed or later noticed in America."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1360011309#11_2282375319"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1360011309#11_2282375319","document":"  ... There is also an illustration of  Izzy Sanabria in a cartoon form with an announcement” a new Salsa music TV show  on WXTU channel 41, premiering Saturday, November 17,1973 at 6:30 pm.” In the  same issue is a photo ad of the DJ Polito Vega which reads “100% Salsa WBNX  Mon-Fri 7:30 to 9:30 p.m.” In issue number 12, February, 1974, there is a full  page ad of the Latin Music Festival Musical number five, with the names Celia  Cruz, Ray Barretto, Johnny Pacheco, Tipica 73, Machito Orquestra and the Apollo  Sound. Not once was salsa mentioned. In March, 1974, Mexicana Records released Rey  Roig’s LP Otra Vezin which Julian Llano sings Pescao en Salsa. During the same month, Fania Records released Larry Harlow’s Salsa,  recorded November 26 and 27, 1973. This album placed Harlow among the top five  most popular bandleaders and the LP enjoyed enormous sales. After this, mostly  every recording of Afro-Cuban rhythms and anything that was exciting in Latin  music was labeled salsa and the anglo market which had abandoned the music went  the cha cha cha followed the mambo popularity in 1956, came back into the fold. In Billboard’s magazine June 12, 1976 issue dedicated to Latin music, there was  a 24 page supplement magazine called “Salsa Explosion.”… If what is written here is accepted as its best  evidence, then it appears that Jimmy Sabater coined the word salsa to mean  uptempo Latin music. Cal Tjader’s Soul Sauceand Santana’s Oye Como  Va gave the salsa movement thrust and its beginning was with the Mexicans in  San Francisco.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"14/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1360011309#11_2282375319","relevance":3,"reason":"The full document gives direct supporting evidence for why salsa was not noticed by mainstream America: before the mid-1970s the term was not consistently used, and the Anglo market had abandoned Afro-Cuban/Latin music after the mambo/cha-cha popularity faded; later, labeling exciting Latin music as 'salsa' brought that market back.","learned":"Besides linguistic/cultural barriers, another explanation is that the Anglo market had lost interest in Latin music after the mambo/cha-cha boom, and salsa only gained notice after the term was applied broadly in the 1970s."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2480","reason":"The current query produced a strong independent salsa-music history source about the Anglo market and salsa's delayed notice; continue to find more similar grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2480"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_2943900068#3_3253974387","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2480","rank":2,"snippet":"<cover>salsa music, and jazz. Sapo, Santana, Malo, War, Tierra, El Chicano and other Chicano ' Latin Rock ' groups follow this approach with their fusions of R&B, Jazz, and Caribbean sounds. 3) A third style is the Latino punk that appeared in the 1970s. History The Champs In places such as San Antonio, Los Angeles, the San Francisco Bay area, and Dallas and Houston, Texas, the African-American audience was very important to aspiring Latino musicians, and this kept their music wedded to authentic R&B. DJ Dick Hugg (aka Huggie Boy) and radio station KRLA 1110 played a big role in promoting this music. Chicano rock music was also influenced by the Doo-wop genre, an example being the song \" Angel Baby \" by the Chicana fronted group Rosie and the Originals . Don Tosti's Pachuco Boogie, recorded in 1948, was the first Chicano million-selling record, a swing tune featuring a Spanish lylics, using hipster slang called Calo. Lalo Guerrero arrived in Los Angeles in the late 1930s and found that L.A is \"bursting with ambition\". Lalo and his friend captured their spirit in music by mixing swing and boogie woogie in a cross-cultural, dialog between African American, </cover>"}}
