{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_131681369#7_215654400","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage is a salsa history article that directly supports why salsa was not noticed earlier in America: the term was only really unleashed and popularized after Fania Records released Larry Harlow's 1974 album, after which Afro-Cuban/Latin rhythms were marketed as salsa and gained notoriety in the Anglo market, reinforced by Billboard's 1976 'Salsa Explosion' supplement.","learned":"Another direct explanation is that salsa did not gain Anglo-American notice until the mid-1970s, when Fania/Larry Harlow popularized the label 'salsa' and Billboard promoted the 'Salsa Explosion.'"},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2483","reason":"This query found a strong independent salsa-history result using Billboard/Fania/Anglo-market wording; continue to find additional grade-3 documents with the same explanation."}}
