{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The Billboard/Salsa Explosion query was exhausted after one new strong salsa-history result; the remaining results were already-judged segments.","learned":"The Fania/Larry Harlow/Billboard 'Salsa Explosion' explanation appears promising but may require variant wording to find more independent pages."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"gained notoriety\" \"anglo-market\" fania)","reason":"Search the distinctive wording from the new grade-3 salsa-history result to find additional documents explaining salsa's delayed notice in the Anglo/American market after Fania popularized the term."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"gained notoriety\" \"anglo-market\" fania)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_131681369#6_215652961","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2484","rank":1,"snippet":"until the 1960s. One of the early salsa albums was the Cal Tjader Quintet plus 5’s Cal Tjader Soul Sauce in which the cover donned a fork on a plate of red beans and chili alongside an opened bottle of Tabasco sauce. Many Mexicans in San Francisco began using the term salsa to describe Tjader’s brand of music. Tjader’s music spread to other cities including Los Angeles and the East Coast. This was the start of Latin music being aired in different formats on radio stations across the country. It was in 1974 that <cover>Fania Records released Larry Harlow’s Salsa. Harlow became very popular and his album enjoyed tremendous sales. This really unleashed the term salsa and popularized it. After this, almost all Afro-Cuban rhythms and much of what was deemed exciting in Latin music acquired the term salsa. It also gained notoriety in the anglo-market.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 2641.000000 , \"title\": \"History of Salsa Dancing – The Origins of Salsa Dancing : Salsa Dancing Addict\" , \"url\": \"http://www.salsa-dancing-addict.com/history-of-salsa.html\" }"}}
