{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#0_1535657338","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage is about Latin music's long but often under-recognized presence in the U.S. mainstream and says Anglophone music media can make Latin pop seem suddenly new, but it is not specifically dedicated to salsa or a direct explanation of why salsa was unnoticed.","learned":"A broader explanation for Latin music being perceived as newly noticed in America is Anglophone music media and new listeners overlooking its long U.S. chart history."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#0_1535657338","reason":"The snippet suggests a media-recognition explanation for Latin music being unnoticed in America; the full document may mention salsa directly or provide stronger supporting evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#0_1535657338"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#0_1535657338","document":"50 Greatest Latin Pop Songs: From 'Bonito' to 'Despacito' - Rolling Stone ... 50 Greatest Latin Pop Songs 50 Greatest Latin Pop Songs Rolling Stone chronicles Latin America’s most influential pop songs, from the 1950s to now Benny Moré, \\\"Bonito y Sabroso\\\" (1951) Ritchie Valens \\\"La Bamba\\\" (1958) Sergio Mendes and Brasil '66, \\\"Mas Que Nada\\\" (1966) Santana, \\\"Oye Como Va\\\" (1970) Héctor Lavoe, \\\"El Periódico de Ayer\\\" (1976) Rubén Blades and Willie Colón, \\\"Plástico\\\" (1978) Juan Gabriel, \\\"Querida\\\" (1984) Rocío Dúrcal, \\\"Amor Eterno\\\" (1984) Daniela Romo, \\\"Yo No Te Pido la Luna\\\" (1984) Los Tigres del Norte, \\\"La Jaula de Oro\\\" (1984) Gloria Estefan and Miami Sound Machine, \\\"Conga\\\" (1985) Ana Gabriel, \\\"Ay Amor\\\" (1987) Caifanes, \\\"La Negra Tomasa\\\" (1988) Luis Miguel, \\\"La Incondicional\\\" (1988) Timbiriche, \\\"Tu y Yo Somos Uno Mismo\\\" (1988) Gloria Trevi, \\\"Dr. Psiquiatra\\\" (1989) Kaoma, \\\"Lambada\\\" (1989) Los Prisioneros, \\\"Tren al Sur\\\" (1990) Selena, \\\"Como la Flor\\\" (1989) El General, \\\"Tu Pum Pum\\\" (1991) Juan Luis Guerra, \\\"Burbujas de Amor\\\" (1991) Maná, \\\"Oye Mi Amor\\\" (1992) Los Fabulosos Cadillacs, \\\"Matador\\\" (1993) Proyecto Uno, \\\"El Tiburón\\\" (1993) Carlos Vives, \\\"La Gota Fría\\\" (1993) La India, \\\"Ese Hombre\\\" (1994) Ricky Martin, \\\"María (Pablo Flores Remix)\\\" (1995) Los Ángeles Azules \\\"Cómo Te Voy A Olvidar\\\" (1996) Elvis Crespo, \\\"Suavemente\\\" (1998) Jennifer Lopez, \\\"Waiting For Tonight\\\" (1999) Celia Cruz, \\\"La Negra Tiene Tumbao\\\" (2001) Aventura, \\\"Obsesión\\\" (2002) Juanes, \\\"A Dios le Pido\\\" (2002) Tego Calderón, \\\"Pa' Que Retozen\\\" (2002) Café Tacvba, \\\"Eres\\\" (2003) Ivy Queen, \\\"Quiero Bailar\\\" (2003) Julieta Venegas, \\\"Algo Esta Cambiando\\\" (2003) Daddy Yankee, \\\"Gasolina\\\" (2004) Grupo Climax, \\\"Za Za Za (Mesa Que Mas Aplauda)\\\" (2004) Calle 13, \\\"Atrévete-Te-Te\\\" (2005) Shakira feat. Wyclef Jean, \\\"Hips Don't Lie\\\" (2006) Bomba Estéreo, \\\"Fuego\\\" (2008) Don Omar feat. Lucenzo, \\\"Danza Kuduro\\\" (2010) 3BallMTY, \\\"Inténtalo\\\" (2011) Alex Anwandter, \\\"Cómo Puedes Vivir Contigo Mismo?\\\" (2011) Marc Anthony, \\\"Vivir Mi Vida\\\" (2013) Enrique Iglesias feat. Sean Paul, Descemer Bueno, Gente De Zona, \\\"Bailando\\\" (2014) J Balvin, \\\"Ginza\\\" (2015) Pitbull feat. Sensato, Osmani Garcia and Dayami La Musa, \\\"El Taxi\\\" (2015) Luis Fonsi feat. Daddy Yankee, \\\"Despacito\\\" (2017)  ... 50 Greatest Latin Pop Songs: From 'Bonito' to 'Despacito' - Rolling Stone Home Music Music Latin Lists July 9, 2018 10:12AM ET 50 Greatest Latin Pop Songs Rolling Stone chronicles Latin America’s most influential pop songs, from the 1950s to now By Suzy Exposito & Andrew Casillas & Isabela Raygoza & John Ochoa & Marjua Estevez With Latin pop getting heightened visibility in the American mainstream this year, it’s time we call for a history lesson. This summer “Latino Gang” Cardi B, Bad Bunny and J Balvin nabbed the Number One spot on the Billboard Hot 100 with their Latin trap hit, “I Like It.” But in sampling the Tony Pabon and Manny Rodriguez-penned single, “I Like It Like That,” this win marks the third time the boogaloo song has cycled through the United States pop chart: first by Pete Rodríguez, whose original recording hit Number 25 in 1967; then again by Tito Puente, Sheila E. and the Blackout All-Stars supergroup in 1996. By reading Anglophone music media, one might think Latin pop’s ubiquity in the United States is a sudden one – but it’s hardly as recent a phenomenon as new listeners believe. From the Cuban mambo craze of the 1950s to the global virality of “Despacito,” Latin American music has been a fixture of popular music around the world so long as it’s been recorded. Just ask Romeo Santos and the Bronx-based bachata group Aventura, whose 2002 single “Obsesión” scored Number Ones across France, Italy and Germany before the United States caught on. Encompassing everything from salsa to rock en español, Latin pop is a constantly evolving genre colored by the traditions, migrations and innovations of Latinx people in spite of all odds.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"22/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#0_1535657338","relevance":2,"reason":"The full Rolling Stone article is a broad Latin pop history list, not dedicated to salsa, but it provides supporting context for why Latin genres may seem unnoticed in America: Anglophone music media and new listeners often overlook Latin music's long U.S. presence. Salsa is included only as part of Latin pop's range.","learned":"A broader media-recognition explanation is that Anglophone U.S. music media can make Latin music seem newly visible even though Latin styles, including salsa, have long histories in and around the American mainstream."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"anglophone\" \"music media\" \"united states\")","reason":"Search the media-recognition angle with salsa-specific terms to find documents directly explaining that salsa or Latin music was unnoticed in America because Anglophone media overlooked it."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"anglophone\" \"music media\" \"united states\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#1_1535661856","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2502","rank":2,"snippet":"De Zona, \"Bailando\" (2014) J Balvin, \"Ginza\" (2015) Pitbull feat. Sensato, Osmani Garcia and Dayami La Musa, \"El Taxi\" (2015) Luis Fonsi feat. Daddy Yankee, \"Despacito\" (2017) \" , \"segment\": \"then again by Tito Puente, Sheila E. and the Blackout All-Stars supergroup in 1996. By reading <cover>Anglophone music media, one might think Latin pop’s ubiquity in the United States is a sudden one – but it’s hardly as recent a phenomenon as new listeners believe. From the Cuban mambo craze of the 1950s to the global virality of “Despacito,” Latin American music has been a fixture of popular music around the world so long as it’s been recorded. Just ask Romeo Santos and the Bronx-based bachata group Aventura, whose 2002 single “Obsesión” scored Number Ones across France, Italy and Germany before the United States caught on. Encompassing everything from salsa </cover>to rock en español, Latin pop is a constantly evolving genre colored by the traditions, migrations and innovations of Latinx people in spite of all odds. Some of the most famous Latin pop songs have survived military dictatorships, war, famine and natural disasters – and they still hold up in spite of passing ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#0_1535657338","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"22/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_735635025#1_1535661856","relevance":2,"reason":"This is another segment of the Rolling Stone Latin pop history article. It gives useful broader support that Anglophone music media and new U.S. listeners often overlook Latin music's long presence, and it includes salsa within Latin pop, but it is not dedicated to salsa or a direct answer to why salsa itself was unnoticed in America.","learned":"Anglophone U.S. music media may make Latin music, including salsa, seem newly visible by overlooking its long-standing presence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"mambo craze\" \"american\" (+ waned ended abandoned))","reason":"Search for historical sources explaining salsa's delayed American notice through the decline of the earlier mambo/cha-cha craze and waning Anglo/American interest in Latin music."}}
