{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The query about the mambo craze waning or being abandoned returned no results.","learned":"The specific 'mambo craze' decline phrasing is not productive for finding additional salsa-specific explanations."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"barrio\" \"fania\" \"mainstream\")","reason":"Search for salsa-history sources that explain salsa's delayed American notice through its roots in New York Latino barrios and later Fania-era promotion to broader audiences."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"barrio\" \"fania\" \"mainstream\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_780071656#8_1573820567","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2504","rank":1,"snippet":"<cover>salsa, Cuban-born Celia Cruz brought a larger-than-life, almost matriarchal persona to a male-dominated genre during the Fania era. With raw, undiluted vocal prowess, a chispa (spark) that spread like a solar flare, and an innate sense of rhythm and swing informed by her African ancestors, Celia was a star unlike any other. To this day, songs like “La Vida es un Carnaval” and “La Negra Tiene Tumbao” are a testament to her place in musical history. Gabriel Olsen/FilmMagic 12 / 31 Item 12 of 31 Daddy Yankee With 2004’s “Gasolina,” Daddy Yankee revved up reggaeton, bringing the underground urban sound of the Puerto Rico barrio to the world. He’s continued to take urban music to new heights ever since. Victor Chavez/WireImage 13 / 31 Item 13 of 31 Carlos Santana Mexican-American icon and guitar great Carlos Santana roared onto the scene with his eponymous group in 1969 at Woodstock. With his fearless fusion of Latin rhythms, jazz and rock, Santana has since been a top-tier reference for American music. A spiritual seeker renowned for his spiraling psychedelic jams, Santana combines artistic integrity with mass commercial success. Michael Ochs Archives 14 / 31 Item </cover>"}}
