{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"popularized\" \"latin new york\" \"fania\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1916138507#14_2861519500","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2506","rank":1,"snippet":"Puente also argued that there is no such thing as salsa but only mambo, rumba, danzon and cha cha cha, etc. According to the late David Melendez, one of the first organizers of the East Coast Salsa Congress and a salsa dancer in New York since the 1970s, the word ‘Salsa’ first referred to the music. The term was coined in the 1970s by young musicians like Hector Lavoe, Larry Harlow, Ray Baretto, Willie Colon, who wanted a different name for the kind of music they were playing. The term ‘<cover>salsa’ was then popularized by Izzy Sanabria, owner of the Latin New York magazine, and Jerry Massuci, owner of Fania </cover>Records. Today, the term ‘salsa’ as we know it, has become synonymous with the dance, yet the dance suffers a “crisis of authenticity” whereby dancers are perpetually disagreeing over what qualifies and does not qualify as “salsa”. Origin of the salsa steps The dance steps currently being danced to salsa music come from the son, but were influenced by many other Cuban dances such as Mambo, Cha cha cha, Guaracha, Changuí, Palo Monte, Rumba, Abakuá, Comparsa and some t\" , \"start_char\": 8473.000000"}}
