{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_987335278#4_1782623624","relevance":2,"reason":"The full PBS segment gives useful direct support for one barrier to salsa's mainstream American notice: salsa artist India was a Latino-market legend but did not cross over, and Tommy Mottola said her rhythms were narrower than Latin pop and did not work in English. Still, it is artist-focused rather than a document dedicated to explaining why salsa as a genre was unnoticed in America.","learned":"Salsa's limited mainstream notice can be linked to English-language crossover barriers: some industry figures considered salsa rhythms less adaptable to English pop than other Latin pop styles."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ salsa \"didn't work in english\" \"mainstream market\")","reason":"Search the distinctive PBS explanation to find more passages or documents directly linking salsa's limited U.S. mainstream notice to English-language crossover problems."}}
